# Everything an agent knows is private, and it is private structurally rather than
# by remembering to check before a commit.
#
# `fleet/` is its own git repository, and this line makes it invisible to this one.
# The old name `agents/` stays listed so a half-renamed checkout can never leak it.
# Nothing under it can be staged here: `git add -A` at this root does not descend into
# an ignored directory, so there is no version of "I forgot" that publishes a note.
#
# The privacy gate in `kb` still works, and that is not an accident. It asks
# `git ls-files` from inside each agent, which resolves to the repository at `fleet/`
# and answers with paths relative to the agent. Measured before this layout was
# adopted, because the previous version of this rule was asserted without being run and
# was wrong.
#
# What this repository publishes: the system in `tools/`, and the shape an agent has,
# which `kb init` generates. Not one line of what anybody fed their agents.
/agents/
/fleet/

# Build output.
target/

# The derived index, wherever it lands. Disposable by ADR-0003.
.kb/

# The recall loss log. Ignored because it holds the user's real questions,
# verbatim, and a question can be as private as an answer: a miss against a
# health base is a health question. What gets committed is the alias line a miss
# eventually earns, never the miss.
kb-misses.txt

# The misroute log and the alias refusal log, ignored for exactly the reason above and
# added in the same change that created them, rather than after a `git status` caught
# them sitting untracked at the root.
#
# kb-misroutes.txt holds the message that was routed wrong, verbatim, plus the reporting
# agent's own words about why. That is the user's question and a second agent's reading of
# it, so it is more private base content than the miss log, not less.
#
# kb-alias-rejections.txt holds a proposed alias line and the reason the gate refused it.
# Both sides of an alias are vocabulary lifted from a real question and from a real note,
# so a refusal quotes the base twice.
kb-misroutes.txt
kb-alias-rejections.txt

# The refusal record, ignored for exactly the reason above and noticed only when a hook
# started producing it unattended. A refusal reason quotes the note it refused and the file
# it collided with, verbatim and in whatever language they were written in, so this file is
# private base content wearing a tooling name. It appeared untracked at the repository root
# on the first automatic run, which is one `git add` away from publishing somebody's base.
kb-rejections.txt

# The objection round ledger, ignored for the same reason and in the same change that
# created it. `kb panel` writes one row per fact about a piece under review: what a
# reviewer objected to, what the owner refused, and the reason for the refusal in the
# owner's own words. That is work in progress plus a second agent's criticism of it, so
# it is private base content in a file named after tooling. The assembled constitutions
# it writes beside it live under `.kb/`, which is already ignored and already disposable.
kb-rounds.txt

# The marker that stops two promotion runs overlapping. Normally it exists for the minutes
# a run takes and then it is gone. It survives a crash on purpose, so the next run can see
# that one died, and a crashed run should not also leave something in `git status` that
# looks like work.
.kb-promote.lock

# Generated by tauri_build on every build of the tray, and it was tracked: 357 KB of
# JSON, two of the four files byte identical. The intent to ignore it was already
# written down in the wrong repository, at fleet/zed/.gitignore, against a path that
# does not exist there.
tools/tray/src-tauri/gen/

# Python bytecode. Derived from the .py beside it and regenerated on every run,
# so by ADR-0003 it was never source. It was also tracked, and the marshalled
# header embeds the absolute path of the machine that compiled it, which is a
# home directory and a username published as a side effect of running a script.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Whisper model cache and generated transcripts: derived, large, and never
# somebody else useful. The source of truth is the video.
tools/scribe/transcripts/

# The anchored rules above protect only the repository root. A fleet moved, cloned or
# vendored anywhere below it deserves the same invisibility, and an anchored pattern
# with a leading slash matches nowhere else. Same reasoning for the eye's screenshots,
# which default to a shots/ folder beside whatever sheet invoked them.
fleet/
agents/
shots/

# Per-user runtime permissions written by the harness on this machine, never shared.
.claude/settings.local.json

# LongMemEval datasets: 280MB of third-party benchmark data, fetched by URL in
# benchmarks/longmemeval/README.md, never vendored.
benchmarks/longmemeval/data/

# Maintainer tooling: on the machine, not in the product.
#
# scribe carries a recording of Richard's voice to the blog. It writes into
# site/frontend/content/posts, runs `kb commit` and pushes, which is an act only
# somebody with write access to this repository can perform, so a stranger who
# clones Ulpia has neither a use for it nor a way to run it. The rule that put it
# here is not "is this useful" but "does a stranger who wants to use, verify or
# contribute to Ulpia need it". Same arrangement as fleet/, same reason.
#
# Untracked with `git rm --cached` rather than deleted, so the files survive on
# the machine that needs them. And the part that matters more than the rule: this
# takes it out of the tree, NOT out of the published history. Anyone who clones
# can still read it at an older commit. This is tidiness, not erasure.
/tools/scribe/
